WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.900
Configuration should be separate from source code, and secrets should be separate from ordinary configuration.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:17.800
Connection strings, API keys, certificates, signing keys, tokens, and deployment credentials need stronger handling than feature flags or display settings.

3
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:25.260
They should not live in repositories, source files, screenshots, build logs, or diagnostic output.

4
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:30.100
Use supported secret-management mechanisms for the platform and deployment environment.

5
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:41.320
That may mean managed secret stores, environment-specific configuration providers, cloud identity, certificate stores, deployment variables, or identity-provider integrations.

6
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:48.700
The important point is that secrets can be controlled, rotated, audited, and removed without rewriting application code.

7
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:54.820
Development, test, staging, and production settings should be treated differently.

8
00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.600
A development shortcut should not silently become a production assumption.

9
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:09.980
Configuration review should confirm that diagnostic settings, test credentials, local endpoints, permissive flags, and sample values do not ship into higher-trust environments.

10
00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:17.860
Sensitive values can leak through logs, exceptions, telemetry, crash reports, support bundles, and debug pages.

11
00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.640
Redaction should be deliberate.

12
00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:31.500
Code that logs configuration objects, headers, claims, request bodies, or exception details should be reviewed for accidental exposure of keys, tokens, and private data.

13
00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:34.660
Data minimization is also defensive programming.

14
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:38.600
Do not collect sensitive data unless the application needs it.

15
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.180
Do not retain it longer than necessary.

16
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.360
Restrict access to what remains.

17
00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:51.380
The less sensitive data the system handles, the smaller the consequence of mistakes in logs, storage, backups, and support workflows.
